urns `false`, so the composite happens to be right. But the guard exists to short-circuit `undefined` (since `JSON.parse(undefined)` throws), which means the author was aware the value might be a string yet still wrote a raw truthiness check. Any value that isn't valid JSON — `ENABLE_DEBUG_LOGS=yes`, `=1 ` with trailing space, `=on` — passes the `&amp;&amp;` and then **throws `SyntaxError` out of `configureLogger`**, at logger-construction time.\n\n**Actual impact:** Debug/console/file logging toggles documented in the env-var table are **always at their hardcoded defaults** (`enableDebugLogs = true`, `enableFileLogging = true`, `enableConsoleLogging = true`, line 17) regardless of `config.json`. Note the defaults also contradict the docs: docs say `ENABLE_DEBUG_LOGS` defaults to `false`, the code defaults it to `true`. So debug logging is silently **always-on** in production, writing to `${src_path}/logs/debug/` (line 23).\n\n---\n\n### 5. `NODE_SERVER_PORT` read without parsing, in a context whose type flips depending on load order\n\n**File:line:** `/Users/mayansharma/Documents/1mg/catalyst-core/packages/catalyst-core/src/server/expressServer.js:139`\n**Expression:** `const port = process.env.NODE_SERVER_PORT ?? 3005`\n**Env var:** `NODE_SERVER_PORT`\n\n**Why it's suspicious:** No `parseInt`. The sibling call site does parse it — `/Users/mayansharma/Documents/1mg/catalyst-core/packages/catalyst-core/src/vite/vite.config.js:507`: `port: process.env.NODE_SERVER_PORT ? parseInt(process.env.NODE_SERVER_PORT) : 3005` — so the codebase is inconsistent about the same variable.\n\nBecause of the `loadEnvironmentVariables` swap, `port` here is a **number** (`3005` from `config.json`) on the normal path, but a **string** if the var ever comes from a real shell env (e.g. a container platform injecting `NODE_SERVER_PORT`, which would then also be clobbered — it isn't in `filterKeys`). `app.listen({ port })` (line 274) tolerates both, so this is latent rather than actively br